{
  "id": "phrases-slang",
  "title": "Phrases, idioms and slang",
  "priority": 3,
  "order": 10,
  "explanation": "Ready-made phrases carry a lot of a conversation, and they are what make you sound less like a textbook. Three groups here.\n\n**Everyday phrases** (neutral): je suis d'accord, on s'est mis d'accord, ça va aller, ne le prends pas mal, ça vaut le coup, c'est la vie, and so on. Learn them as whole chunks, not word by word.\n\n**Idioms**: avoir des fourmis dans les jambes (pins and needles, literally ants), aller de l'avant (move on), lâcher prise (let go), c'est donnant donnant (give and take), sur les rails (on track). Careful with the two \"better\" sayings: *mieux vaut tard que jamais* is better late than never, and *mieux vaut tôt que tard* is better sooner than later. The notes had them crossed. French also likes \"comme\" comparisons: gavé comme une oie.\n\n**Slang and verlan**: verlan flips syllables. Ouf is fou backwards (c'est ouf = that's crazy), chelou is louche backwards (c'est chelou = that's dodgy). Also parano, cucul (soppy), nunuche (a bit dim, mildly unkind), en vrai, bref, nul, c'est la fête du slip (it's chaos, informal). The vulgar items are hidden unless you switch them on in Settings.",
